     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Whether duplicate proposals must create a new binding identity instead of returning the existing binding",
   "members": [
    "r1.01",
    "r3.01"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "When an equal proposal already exists, must this call always record and return a newly proposed binding with a newly minted identity, rather than return the existing binding?",
    "When an otherwise equal proposal already exists, must this call mint, record and return a new binding rather than return the existing binding?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "?"
   ],
   "witnessed_by": [
    "r1.01",
    "r3.01"
   ],
   "id": "T2",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "answered",
    "quotes": [
     "`binding_id`: minted at random by the kernel when proposed; two proposals with\n  equal content are two bindings;",
     "A proposal that passes every check of rule 5 but gives its input and output\n   ports out of name order is recorded, and the answer is that one binding with\n   its ports in name order; another proposal of the same operation already in\n   the store is not the answer. A proposal failing a check of rule 5 is refused\n   whatever its port order."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "State 1 makes equal proposals separate bindings, and State 2 requires the newly recorded binding—not an existing proposal—to be returned.",
    "decision_owner": "State 2",
    "options": [
     "Always create and return a distinct proposed binding, even when an equal proposal already exists.",
     "Return the existing equal binding and record nothing new.",
     "Create a new binding but return the previously stored equal binding."
    ],
